ant any truce and let's get our fees  out of the picture you know and so that  has created its own difficulties but I  think that the Libyan people I mean they  don't have to destroy their country  before they come to peace there should  be some dialogue that goes on you know I  think the period for air strikes is  relatively over I mean the threats to  the citizens is a bit more updated and  so we should look at the opportunity for  them to dialogue and try and resolve the  issue  otherwise if it continues like this I  mean by the time you know peace returns  that country would have been completely  obliterated now in Cote d'Ivoire  you were very your President issued a  statement and he also said that our  policies of peace he stayed out of Cote  d'Ivoire has that affected your  relationship with terror  no we have a fantastic relation which  could be why is our neighbor and so our  destinies are tied to each other we  would have wished that the issue was  resolved more amicably and peacefully  rather than through the force of arms  unfortunately the resolution eventually  came through you know fighting and so we  think that Cote d'Ivoire has the  opportunity to make a fresh start and a  lot to depend on what our government you  know he needs to reach out and create a  government of national unity and start a  healing process in code avoir codifies  problems  have an ethnic and religious dimension  and I think that how president whatever  handles it going forward is going to be  important  Ghana our president was at his  inauguration and we placed any support  they wanted to help you know could've  won you know become the nation that it  was once again living at peace with  itself and developing the quality of  lives of its people  and finally globalization has been very  positive for Ghana overall I think  especially because given Ghana's natural  resources gold cocoa and now oil it is  must be doing pretty well within the  given market of commodity prices rising  now as you have said yourself that  having these natural uses could also be  cars so how do you hope to prevent Ghana  becoming like another Nigeria with with  a lot of resources but lot of problem as  well how do you avoid that we have been  very lucky especially with the oil and  gas industry because of the timing at  which we discovered you know Ghana's oil  and gas potential has given us the  opportunity to learn from the best  experiences and from the west  experiences and let me say on record  that both experiences I mean countries  that have gone through both experiences  have been very generous in sharing the  experience with us countries like Norway  you know which are some of the best  practices in how to manage an oil and  gas industry have been absolutely  absolutely fantastic in assisting Ghana  to develop our legislation and so on and  so forth and countries like Nigeria  which have had you know not good so good  experiences with oil have also been  fantastic and generous in sharing their  experience with us we've had several  Nigerian resource people come and talk  at oil and gas conferences in Ghana and  analyse you know what you know problems  they went through and all that and I  don't absolutely blame them Nigeria had  nobody to learn from they discovered oil  and gas in the 19th  there were no other African countries or  other developing countries that I've had  this resource and so they've had to  learn from their own experience and  develop their own experience you know  but I must say that they have made quite  rapid progress they have a strong local  industry that services the oil and gas  industry that we don't have in Ghana you  know and so they are telling us look at  local content look at local content  because you must increase the multiplier  effect of the industry in your economy  it's not enough to just get the money  from the oil sales and all that and  develop huge infrastructure but how you  integrates the industry backwards into  your economy is critical and so that's  one red flag decreed for us and so right  at the beginning of the industry we are  passing a look of content bill that  forces the oil companies to look for  expertise locally so that's a good thing  but all you know I mean we do have a lot  of Natural Resources bauxite manganese  you know gold you know and cocoa and so  on and so forth  one of the things we want to avoid is  the Dutch disease so that oil does not  become like the dominant you know sector  of economy and that we channel some of  the oil resources into keeping  agriculture active ensuring food  security for Ghana and so on and so  forth so we've learned from those also  have I think said that you are going to  and here too the extractive industries  transparency initiative  yeah and Ghana is an active member we do  regular assessments to ensure that these  things are being you know applied in in  a transparent manner and so on and so  forth we have regular review teams and  so on and so Ghana is committed to that  and we will uphold it  one advantage Ghana has in all this is  that we have a free society people are  free to criticize we have a vibrant  media that you know is free to write  anything at once and I think that those  are the guarantees you need you know to  ensure transparency going forward on  that very positive note mr. president  thank you very much for talking to us  thank you very much thanks for having me

